window.docContents[112].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-automorphisms-of-cycle-graphs":{"contents":"In isVertexTransitive_cycleGraph we showed that the cycle graph C[n] is\nvertex-transitive, using the rotations cycleGraphRotate d, which send the vertex\nd to 0. We now determine its whole automorphism group. Besides the rotations\nthere is an automorphism given by reflecting the vertices, i.e. sending k to -k\nmodulo n.\n\nThe automorphism of the cycle graph C[n] given by reflecting the vertices./--\nThe automorphism of the cycle graph C[n] given by reflecting the vertices.\n-/\n@[simps]\ndef cycleGraphReflect {n : ℕ} : C[n] ≃g C[n] where\n  toFun k := -k\n  invFun k := -k\n  left_inv k := by simp\n  right_inv k := by simp\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    match n with\n    | 0 | 1 => simp\n    | n + 2 =>\n      intro a b\n      change -a - -b = 1 ∨ -b - -a = 1 ↔ a - b = 1 ∨ b - a = 1\n      simp [sub_eq_add_neg, add_comm, or_comm]\n\n\nIf e : C[n + 2] ≃g C[n + 2] is an automorphism of the cycle graph and\ne 1 - e 0 = 1 then e is a rotation, namely the one sending the vertex e.symm 0\nto 0. We work with C[n + 2] throughout since cycleGraph_adj only\ndescribes adjacency in C[n + 2]. The statements would remain (trivially) true for\nC[n + 1], but the classification of automorphisms below is false for C[0]:\nthe empty map is an automorphism, yet Fin 0 has no element to witness the\nexistential.\n\nlemma aut_cycleGraph_eq_rotate {n : ℕ} (e : C[n + 2] ≃g C[n + 2])\n    (h : e 1 - e 0 = 1) : e = cycleGraphRotate (e.symm 0) := by\n  let i := e 0\n  have hstep : e 1 = 1 + i := by grind\n  have hall : ∀ (j : ℕ) (hj : j < n + 2),\n      e ⟨j, hj⟩ = ⟨j, hj⟩ + i := by\n    intro j\n    induction j using Nat.twoStepInduction with\n    | zero => simp [i]\n    | one  =>  simpa [add_comm] using hstep\n    | more j ihj ihj1 =>\n      intro hj2\n      have hadj : C[n + 2].Adj (e ⟨j + 2, hj2⟩) (e ⟨j + 1, by grind⟩) :=\n        e.toHom.map_adj (by\n          rw [cycleGraph_adj]\n          exact Or.inl (Fin.ext (by rw [Fin.sub_val_of_le (by simp)]; simp)))\n      have hs : (⟨j + 1, by grind⟩ : Fin (n + 2)) = 1 + ⟨j, by grind⟩ := by\n        apply Fin.ext\n        change j + 1 = (1 + j) % (n + 2)\n        rw [Nat.mod_eq_of_lt (by grind), add_comm]\n      have hnext : (⟨j + 2, hj2⟩ : Fin (n + 2)) = 1 + ⟨j + 1, by grind⟩ := by\n        apply Fin.ext\n        change j + 2 = (1 + (j + 1)) % (n + 2)\n        rw [Nat.mod_eq_of_lt (by grind), add_comm 1]\n      rcases hadj with hadj | hadj\n      · grind\n      · have heq : e ⟨j + 2, hj2⟩ = e ⟨j, by grind⟩ := by grind\n        have : j + 2 = j := Fin.mk.inj (e.toEquiv.injective heq)\n        grind\n  have h0 : e (-i) = 0 := by\n    simpa [i] using hall (-i).val (-i).isLt\n  have hsymm : e.symm 0 = -i := e.symm_apply_eq.2 h0.symm\n  rw [hsymm]\n  ext k\n  simpa [i, sub_neg_eq_add] using congrArg Fin.val (hall k.val k.isLt)\n\n\nIf instead e 0 - e 1 = 1 then e is a rotation followed by the reflection. To\nsee this note that f = cycleGraphReflect ∘ e satisfies f 1 - f 0 = 1, so f is\na rotation by the previous lemma, and hence e = cycleGraphReflect ∘ f is as\nclaimed (the reflection is an involution).\n\nlemma aut_cycleGraph_eq_rotate_trans_reflect {n : ℕ} (e : C[n + 2] ≃g C[n + 2])\n    (h : e 0 - e 1 = 1) :\n    e = (cycleGraphRotate (e.symm 0)).trans cycleGraphReflect := by\n  let f := e.trans cycleGraphReflect\n  have hf : f 1 - f 0 = 1 := by\n    simpa [f, sub_eq_add_neg, add_comm] using h\n  have hsymm : e.symm 0 = f.symm 0 := e.symm_apply_eq.2 (by simp [f])\n  rw [hsymm, ← aut_cycleGraph_eq_rotate f hf]\n  ext k\n  simp [f]\n\n\nHence any automorphism of the cycle graph C[n + 2] is either a rotation or a\nrotation followed by reflection.\n\nlemma aut_cycleGraph {n : ℕ} (e : C[n + 2] ≃g C[n + 2]) : ∃ i : Fin (n + 2),\n    e = cycleGraphRotate i ∨ e = (cycleGraphRotate i).trans cycleGraphReflect := by\n  have h0 : C[n + 2].Adj (e 0) (e 1) :=\n    e.toHom.map_adj (by grind [cycleGraph_adj])\n  rcases h0 with h | h\n  · exact ⟨_, Or.inr (aut_cycleGraph_eq_rotate_trans_reflect e h)⟩\n  · exact ⟨_, Or.inl (aut_cycleGraph_eq_rotate e h)⟩\n\n\nFor n ≥ 3 the automorphism group of C[n] is the dihedral group\nDihedralGroup n, which is why we state the result for C[n + 3]. For n < 3 it\nfails for trivial reasons:\n\n* C[0] has vertex type Fin 0, hence exactly one automorphism, while\nDihedralGroup 0 is infinite;* C[1] has one vertex, hence one automorphism, while DihedralGroup 1 has two\nelements;* C[2] is a single edge, with two automorphisms, while DihedralGroup 2 has four\nelements.\n\nOur rotations match Mathlib's conventions for DihedralGroup exactly: r i\ncorresponds to cycleGraphRotate i and, since sr i = sr 0 * r i, the reflection\nsr i corresponds to cycleGraphRotate i followed by the reflection. In both cases\nthe index i is the vertex sent to 0, i.e. e.symm 0.\n\n#source DihedralGroup\n\n\nMultiplicativity is easiest to check in the direction from DihedralGroup (n + 3),\nwhere it amounts to four short computations with rotations and reflections, so we\nbuild the isomorphism in that direction and then take its inverse.\n\nThe automorphism group of the cycle graph C[n + 3] is isomorphic to the\ndihedral group of order 2 * (n + 3)./-- The automorphism group of the cycle graph C[n + 3] is isomorphic to the\ndihedral group of order 2 * (n + 3). -/\ndef autCycleGraphMulEquivDihedral (n : ℕ) :\n    (C[n + 3] ≃g C[n + 3]) ≃* DihedralGroup (n + 3) :=\n  MulEquiv.symm\n    { toFun := fun d => match d with\n        | .r i => cycleGraphRotate ((ZMod.finEquiv (n + 3)).symm i)\n        | .sr i => (cycleGraphRotate ((ZMod.finEquiv (n + 3)).symm i)).trans\n            cycleGraphReflect\n      invFun e := if e 1 - e 0 = 1 then .r (ZMod.finEquiv (n + 3) (e.symm 0))\n        else .sr (ZMod.finEquiv (n + 3) (e.symm 0))\n      left_inv d := by\n        have hneg : (-1 : Fin (n + 3)) ≠ 1 := by simp [Fin.ext_iff]\n        rcases d with i | i <;> simp [hneg]\n      right_inv e := by\n        by_cases h : e 1 - e 0 = 1\n        · simpa [h] using (aut_cycleGraph_eq_rotate e h).symm\n        · have h0 : C[n + 3].Adj (e 0) (e 1) :=\n            e.toHom.map_adj (by grind [cycleGraph_adj])\n          rw [cycleGraph_adj] at h0\n          simpa [h] using\n            (aut_cycleGraph_eq_rotate_trans_reflect e (h0.resolve_right h)).symm\n      map_mul' := by\n        rintro (i | i) (j | j) <;> ext k <;>\n          simp [map_add, map_sub, sub_sub, sub_sub_eq_add_sub, add_comm] }\n\n\nCombining this with the isomorphism hypercubeTwoIsoCycleFour : Q[2] ≃g C[4] from\nthe exercises of Maps, the automorphism group of the square\nQ[2] is the dihedral group of order 8. (We identify the automorphism group of\nevery hypercube in The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube.)\n\nThe automorphism group of the square Q[2] is the dihedral group of\norder 8./-- The automorphism group of the square Q[2] is the dihedral group of\norder 8. -/\ndef autHypercubeTwoMulEquivDihedral : (Q[2] ≃g Q[2]) ≃* DihedralGroup 4 :=\n  hypercubeTwoIsoCycleFour.autCongr.trans (autCycleGraphMulEquivDihedral 1)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Automorphism Groups","header":"12.1.2. Automorphisms of Cycle Graphs","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-automorphisms-of-cycle-graphs"},"/Maps/#section-tensor-products":{"contents":"As a further example of a construction using SimpleGraph.comap, we define the\ntensor product of two graphs, and then the tensor product of an indexed family of\ngraphs.\n\nThe tensor product G ⊗g H of graphs on V and W is the SimpleGraph (V × W)\nwhere two vertices (v₁, w₁) and (v₂, w₂) are adjacent iff G.Adj v₁ v₂ and\nH.Adj w₁ w₂./-- The tensor product G ⊗g H of graphs on V and W is the SimpleGraph (V × W)\nwhere two vertices (v₁, w₁) and (v₂, w₂) are adjacent iff G.Adj v₁ v₂ and\nH.Adj w₁ w₂. -/\ndef tensorProd {V W : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (H : SimpleGraph W) :\n    SimpleGraph (V × W) :=\n  G.comap Prod.fst ⊓ H.comap Prod.snd\n\n\nWe introduce the notation G ⊗g H for the tensor product of two graphs.\n\n@[inherit_doc]\nscoped infixl:70 \" ⊗g \" => SimpleGraph.tensorProd\n\n\nAdjacency in the tensor product is given by the adjacency relations of the two\ngraphs. (As for Mathlib's boxProd_adj, we state this as an ↔ lemma, so that it\ncan be used with .1/.2 as well as with rw and simp.)\n\nTensor product P_3\\otimes K_3\n\n@[simp]\nlemma tensorProd_adj {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    {x y : V × W} : (G ⊗g H).Adj x y ↔ G.Adj x.1 y.1 ∧ H.Adj x.2 y.2 := Iff.rfl\n\n\nexample {V W : Type*} {G H} {v₁ v₂ : V} {w₁ w₂ : W} :\n    (G ⊗g H).Adj (v₁, w₁) (v₂, w₂) ↔ G.Adj v₁ v₂ ∧ H.Adj w₁ w₂ := Iff.rfl\n\n\nDecidable adjacency for tensor products follows from decidable adjacency for the two\ngraphs.\n\ninstance {V W : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (H : SimpleGraph W) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    [DecidableRel H.Adj] : DecidableRel (G ⊗g H).Adj :=\n  fun _ _ ↦ decidable_of_iff' _ tensorProd_adj\n\n\nIf x.1 and x.2 have finite neighbourhoods in G and H respectively, then so\ndoes x in G ⊗g H, since its neighbourhood is the product s ×ˢ t of the two\nneighbourhoods. (We state the instance for x : V × W rather than for a pair\n(v, w), since otherwise it would not fire on a general x : V × W.)\n\nIf G and H have finite neighbourhoods at x.fst and x.snd respectively,\nthen their tensor product has a finite neighbourhood at x./-- If G and H have finite neighbourhoods at x.fst and x.snd respectively,\nthen their tensor product has a finite neighbourhood at x. -/\ninstance {V W : Type*} {x : V × W} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet x.fst)] [Fintype (H.neighborSet x.snd)] :\n    Fintype ((G ⊗g H).neighborSet x) :=\n  Fintype.ofFinset (G.neighborFinset x.fst ×ˢ H.neighborFinset x.snd) (by simp)\n\n\nThe degree of a vertex in the tensor product is the product of the degrees of its\ncomponents./-- The degree of a vertex in the tensor product is the product of the degrees of its\ncomponents. -/\nlemma degree_tensorProd {V W : Type*} {x : V × W} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n    {H : SimpleGraph W} [Fintype (G.neighborSet x.fst)]\n    [Fintype (H.neighborSet x.snd)] [Fintype ((G ⊗g H).neighborSet x)] :\n    (G ⊗g H).degree x = G.degree x.fst * H.degree x.snd := by\n  simp_rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree, ← Finset.card_product]\n  congr\n  ext ⟨x, y⟩\n  simp\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps","header":"5.7. Tensor Products*","id":"/Maps/#section-tensor-products"}});